{"id": 32, "ts": 1784202308.809219, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "agent_2", "message": "I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fB is our best provisional choice. As the panel has highlighted, B\u2019s very conscientious nature and strong stress\u2011handling ability directly satisfy operational reliability and stress resilience\u2014both non\u2011negotiable for long\u2011distance flights. His solid technical competence (excellent weather\u2011assessment skill and computer proficiency) further supports the required cognitive demands.\nThe only lingering reservation is his pretentious tone and occasional \u201cwrong tone\u201d in communication. However, we have no concrete incidents showing that this has impaired crew cooperation, and the concern appears less severe than the documented egocentric attitude of A, the \u201cshow\u2011off\u201d and closed\u2011to\u2011new\u2011ideas perception of C, or the hot\u2011tempered, know\u2011it\u2011all, loner image of D. Moreover, C\u2019s impressive technical traits (anticipating dangerous situations, seeing complex connections, excellent spatial vision) are offset by the clear lack of evidence that he can work constructively with a crew, and his resistance to new ideas raises doubts about adaptability. D, despite strong problem\u2011solving and responsibility, also lacks any proven teamwork record and shows temperament issues that could jeopardize cockpit harmony.\nGiven the current evidence, B offers the most balanced profile across all safety\u2011critical criteria, and any communication risk can be mitigated through targeted onboarding and feedback mechanisms. Therefore, I continue to recommend Candidate\u202fB as the leading option while we keep monitoring the social\u2011skill gaps for C and D."}
